 ***Multimodal AI Framework for Head and Neck Lymphoma Classification***

## Cohort Overview

A total of 662 cases were included in the study, comprising aggressive B-cell lymphomas, indolent/small B-cell lymphomas, NK/T-cell lymphomas, and reactive lesions collected from ten 
international institutions across Brazil, Guatemala, Mexico, and the United Kingdom. The dataset incorporated a wide range of anatomical sites and demographic profiles, reflecting real-world 
diagnostic heterogeneity.

# Nuclear Segmentation and Morphometric Analysis

Nuclear segmentation using CellViT++ demonstrated strong performance across heterogeneous histopathological samples:

* **Dice coefficient:** 0.91 (range: 0.86–0.95)
* **Intersection over Union (IoU):** 0.84 (range: 0.78–0.90)

Morphometric analysis revealed biologically meaningful differences between diagnostic categories:

| Category                       | Nuclear Area | Perimeter | Circularity | Eccentricity |
| ------------------------------ | ------------ | --------- | ----------- | ------------ |
| Aggressive B-cell lymphoma     | 23.83        | 19.69     | 0.752       | 0.742        |
| Indolent/small B-cell lymphoma | 20.73        | 18.37     | 0.784       | 0.707        |
| NK/T-cell lymphoma             | 20.12        | 18.17     | 0.755       | 0.758        |
| Reactive lesions               | 20.58        | 18.03     | 0.812       | 0.697        |

Aggressive lymphomas demonstrated larger nuclear dimensions and greater morphometric irregularity, consistent with high-grade biological behavior.

